{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_013zoqvceCZeudAtNrpS5x4Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0182vdDWp7YMZebZcuF6EGan","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","offset":680,"limit":150},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":260,"cache_read_input_tokens":88016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":260},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a061755-a806-479f-90eb-b6112c5e5966","uuid":"3aa52440-41ea-4e8c-868c-30994a2de642"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0182vdDWp7YMZebZcuF6EGan","type":"tool_result","content":"680\t\n681\t<h2>Long-term Housing Strategy</h2>\n682\t<p>Once you've secured your job and work pass, apartment hunting shifts from monthly stays to 12-month leases. <a href=\"/getting-here\">Getting to Pelangi from the Causeway</a> is 10–15 minutes by Grab, so proximity to the hostel signals good transport connections for future housing. Target neighbourhoods: Taman Pelangi, Danga Bay, Larkin. Monthly rents: RM800–1,500 for 1-bedroom private flats in these areas.</p>\n683\t\n684\t<h2>Making the Commute Sustainable</h2>\n685\t<p>Daily commuting is sustainable, not glamorous. The Causeway is reliable. Weather delays (rare) add 10–15 minutes. Strikes or roadwork (annual) are predictable and avoidable. Most commuters find the rhythm within 2 weeks. The financial upside — SGD50,000+ annual savings — justifies the daily 1.5-hour round trip.</p>\n686\t\n687\t<p>Ready to cross the Causeway? <a href=\"/rooms\">Book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> as your first base, and start your cross-border career today.</p>\n688\t`,\n689\t  },\n690\t  {\n691\t    slug: \"gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass\",\n692\t    title: \"5 Gig Jobs for Malaysians Waiting for Singapore Work Approval\",\n693\t    excerpt:\n694\t      \"Stuck in the work pass waiting period? Make money while you wait. Foodpanda, Lalamove, Grab, and more—5 flexible gig economy jobs in Malaysia with realistic earnings.\",\n695\t    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n696\t    category: \"Career & Work\",\n697\t    author: \"Pelangi Team\",\n698\t    readTime: \"9 min read\",\n699\t    tags: [\"gig-economy\", \"malaysia\", \"work\", \"freelance\", \"income\", \"job-seeker\"],\n700\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/gig-jobs.jpg\",\n701\t    content: `\n702\t<h2>Making Money While You Wait</h2>\n703\t<p>Singapore work pass approvals take 4–12 weeks. Pelangi guests often ask: how do I earn during this gap? The answer: Malaysia's gig economy. You can earn RM1,500–4,000/month with zero commitment. Here are 5 proven gig jobs for your transition.</p>\n704\t\n705\t<h2>1. Foodpanda Delivery Rider — RM1,800–3,500/month</h2>\n706\t<p><strong>What:</strong> Deliver food via motorbike, flexible shifts, work 4–12 hours anytime.</p>\n707\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, motorcycle license, own motorbike, Foodpanda app. Age 18+. Background check is instant (10 minutes).</p>\n708\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM5–8 per delivery + tips. Peak hours (lunch 11am–2pm, dinner 6–9pm): RM80–120/day. Part-time: RM1,500–2,500/month. Full-time: RM3,000–3,500/month.</p>\n709\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Instant signup, daily payouts, complete schedule control, stay active. Popular with expats in transition.</p>\n710\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Weather-dependent, traffic stress, no benefits, rating-sensitive, fatigue from long hours.</p>\n711\t\n712\t<h2>2. Lalamove Driver — RM2,000–3,800/month</h2>\n713\t<p><strong>What:</strong> Deliver parcels and documents across city, less frequent than food delivery but higher pay per trip.</p>\n714\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, driving license, own car or motorbike, insurance. Age 18+. Vetting takes 2–3 days.</p>\n715\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM8–15 per delivery (distance-based). Corporate deadlines (end-month): RM20–30 per delivery. Part-time: RM1,800–2,500/month. Full-time: RM3,000–3,800/month.</p>\n716\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Higher per-delivery rate, less traffic pressure (packages don't spoil), flexible shifts, same-day payout.</p>\n717\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Fewer orders than food delivery, vehicle damage liability, parking costs, less suitable for motorbikes.</p>\n718\t\n719\t<h2>3. Grab Ride or GrabFood — RM2,500–4,200/month</h2>\n720\t<p><strong>What:</strong> GrabCar (passenger rides) or GrabFood (delivery). Grab's bigger ecosystem = more orders.</p>\n721\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, driving license (5+ years for GrabCar), own vehicle, car insurance. Age 21+ for rides, 18+ for food.</p>\n722\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> GrabCar: RM3–25 per ride. GrabFood: RM6–12 per delivery. Average 10–15 rides/day = RM80–150/day. Surge pricing (Friday evening, weekends) = 1.5–2x multiplier. Part-time: RM2,000–3,000/month. Full-time: RM3,500–4,200/month.</p>\n723\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Strong brand trust, transparent pay, surge pricing rewards peak work, high volume keeps you busy.</p>\n724\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> GrabCar needs 5-year license, passenger interaction risks (safety at night), fuel costs high, wear-and-tear on car, cancellations hurt earnings.</p>\n725\t\n726\t<h2>4. Part-Time F&B / Cafe Work — RM1,500–2,500/month</h2>\n727\t<p><strong>What:</strong> Barista, bartender, waiter, kitchen help. Flexible shift work, common for gap periods.</p>\n728\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Work visa or Malaysian employment rights, punctuality, basic English for customer roles.</p>\n729\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM8–12/hour + tips. 3–4 shifts/week, 6–8 hours = RM1,500–2,500/month. High-end bars: RM15–20/hour + 10% service split.</p>\n730\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Predictable hourly pay, tips add 20–30%, meals included, social environment, structured schedule.</p>\n731\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Fixed shifts (less flexible), standing fatigue, early/late hours, customer service stress.</p>\n732\t\n733\t<h2>5. Online Freelancing — RM1,200–5,000+/month</h2>\n734\t<p><strong>What:</strong> Virtual assistant, content writing, social media, graphic design, coding, translation. Laptop-based, any time.</p>\n735\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Laptop, stable internet, relevant skills, portfolio, Upwork/Fiverr/Freelancer account.</p>\n736\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> Entry (RM5–10/hr): RM1,200–2,000/month. Mid (RM15–30/hr): RM2,500–4,000/month. Expert (RM30–100/hr+): RM4,000–8,000+/month. Ramp-up takes 2–3 months as reviews build.</p>\n737\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Location-independent, work your pace, scale by reputation, no commute, leverage existing skills.</p>\n738\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Slow start, platform competition, time-zone issues with Western clients, payment delays (30-day holds), self-discipline needed.</p>\n739\t\n740\t<h2>Combining Gigs for Max Income</h2>\n741\t<p>Smart approach: deliver food 5–6 hours/day (RM400–600) + freelance 3–4 hours evenings (RM150–300) = RM550–900/day. Pair active gigs (delivery, rides) with remote work. During slow hours (2–5pm), do freelance projects.</p>\n742\t\n743\t<h2>Tax and Legal</h2>\n744\t<p>Gig income is generally taxable. Keep earnings records. Check your work visa terms—S Pass and Employment Pass typically allow gig work outside your primary job. Confirm with your employer before starting.</p>\n745\t\n746\t<h2>Pelangi Guests & Gap-Filling</h2>\n747\t<p>Many guests stay with us during that 4–12 week limbo. Those who pick up gig work report three wins: (1) funding relocation costs, (2) staying mentally active, (3) natural downtime when returning to Pelangi. Several guests deliver Foodpanda and work from our common areas.</p>\n748\t\n749\t<h2>Next Steps</h2>\n750\t<p>Choose a gig: Foodpanda for ease, freelancing for scalability, F&B for social connection. <a href=\"/rooms\">Book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> as your base while you earn and prepare for Singapore.</p>\n751\t`,\n752\t  },\n753\t];\n754\t\n755\texport const blogPosts: BlogPost[] = rawPosts.map((post) => ({\n756\t  ...post,\n757\t  readingTimeMinutes: computeReadingTime(post.content),\n758\t}));\n759\t\n760\texport function getPostBySlug(slug: string): BlogPost | undefined {\n761\t  return blogPosts.find((post) => post.slug === slug);\n762\t}\n763\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a061755-a806-479f-90eb-b6112c5e5966","uuid":"7ac74d85-3f7e-43a5-b275-b60506958063","timestamp":"2026-04-13T21:12:28.541Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","content":"\n<h2>Long-term Housing Strategy</h2>\n<p>Once you've secured your job and work pass, apartment hunting shifts from monthly stays to 12-month leases. <a href=\"/getting-here\">Getting to Pelangi from the Causeway</a> is 10–15 minutes by Grab, so proximity to the hostel signals good transport connections for future housing. Target neighbourhoods: Taman Pelangi, Danga Bay, Larkin. Monthly rents: RM800–1,500 for 1-bedroom private flats in these areas.</p>\n\n<h2>Making the Commute Sustainable</h2>\n<p>Daily commuting is sustainable, not glamorous. The Causeway is reliable. Weather delays (rare) add 10–15 minutes. Strikes or roadwork (annual) are predictable and avoidable. Most commuters find the rhythm within 2 weeks. The financial upside — SGD50,000+ annual savings — justifies the daily 1.5-hour round trip.</p>\n\n<p>Ready to cross the Causeway? <a href=\"/rooms\">Book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> as your first base, and start your cross-border career today.</p>\n`,\n  },\n  {\n    slug: \"gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass\",\n    title: \"5 Gig Jobs for Malaysians Waiting for Singapore Work Approval\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"Stuck in the work pass waiting period? Make money while you wait. Foodpanda, Lalamove, Grab, and more—5 flexible gig economy jobs in Malaysia with realistic earnings.\",\n    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n    category: \"Career & Work\",\n    author: \"Pelangi Team\",\n    readTime: \"9 min read\",\n    tags: [\"gig-economy\", \"malaysia\", \"work\", \"freelance\", \"income\", \"job-seeker\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/gig-jobs.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>Making Money While You Wait</h2>\n<p>Singapore work pass approvals take 4–12 weeks. Pelangi guests often ask: how do I earn during this gap? The answer: Malaysia's gig economy. You can earn RM1,500–4,000/month with zero commitment. Here are 5 proven gig jobs for your transition.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Foodpanda Delivery Rider — RM1,800–3,500/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> Deliver food via motorbike, flexible shifts, work 4–12 hours anytime.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, motorcycle license, own motorbike, Foodpanda app. Age 18+. Background check is instant (10 minutes).</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM5–8 per delivery + tips. Peak hours (lunch 11am–2pm, dinner 6–9pm): RM80–120/day. Part-time: RM1,500–2,500/month. Full-time: RM3,000–3,500/month.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Instant signup, daily payouts, complete schedule control, stay active. Popular with expats in transition.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Weather-dependent, traffic stress, no benefits, rating-sensitive, fatigue from long hours.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Lalamove Driver — RM2,000–3,800/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> Deliver parcels and documents across city, less frequent than food delivery but higher pay per trip.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, driving license, own car or motorbike, insurance. Age 18+. Vetting takes 2–3 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM8–15 per delivery (distance-based). Corporate deadlines (end-month): RM20–30 per delivery. Part-time: RM1,800–2,500/month. Full-time: RM3,000–3,800/month.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Higher per-delivery rate, less traffic pressure (packages don't spoil), flexible shifts, same-day payout.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Fewer orders than food delivery, vehicle damage liability, parking costs, less suitable for motorbikes.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Grab Ride or GrabFood — RM2,500–4,200/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> GrabCar (passenger rides) or GrabFood (delivery). Grab's bigger ecosystem = more orders.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, driving license (5+ years for GrabCar), own vehicle, car insurance. Age 21+ for rides, 18+ for food.</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> GrabCar: RM3–25 per ride. GrabFood: RM6–12 per delivery. Average 10–15 rides/day = RM80–150/day. Surge pricing (Friday evening, weekends) = 1.5–2x multiplier. Part-time: RM2,000–3,000/month. Full-time: RM3,500–4,200/month.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Strong brand trust, transparent pay, surge pricing rewards peak work, high volume keeps you busy.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> GrabCar needs 5-year license, passenger interaction risks (safety at night), fuel costs high, wear-and-tear on car, cancellations hurt earnings.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Part-Time F&B / Cafe Work — RM1,500–2,500/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> Barista, bartender, waiter, kitchen help. Flexible shift work, common for gap periods.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Work visa or Malaysian employment rights, punctuality, basic English for customer roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM8–12/hour + tips. 3–4 shifts/week, 6–8 hours = RM1,500–2,500/month. High-end bars: RM15–20/hour + 10% service split.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Predictable hourly pay, tips add 20–30%, meals included, social environment, structured schedule.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Fixed shifts (less flexible), standing fatigue, early/late hours, customer service stress.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Online Freelancing — RM1,200–5,000+/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> Virtual assistant, content writing, social media, graphic design, coding, translation. Laptop-based, any time.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Laptop, stable internet, relevant skills, portfolio, Upwork/Fiverr/Freelancer account.</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> Entry (RM5–10/hr): RM1,200–2,000/month. Mid (RM15–30/hr): RM2,500–4,000/month. Expert (RM30–100/hr+): RM4,000–8,000+/month. Ramp-up takes 2–3 months as reviews build.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Location-independent, work your pace, scale by reputation, no commute, leverage existing skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Slow start, platform competition, time-zone issues with Western clients, payment delays (30-day holds), self-discipline needed.</p>\n\n<h2>Combining Gigs for Max Income</h2>\n<p>Smart approach: deliver food 5–6 hours/day (RM400–600) + freelance 3–4 hours evenings (RM150–300) = RM550–900/day. Pair active gigs (delivery, rides) with remote work. During slow hours (2–5pm), do freelance projects.</p>\n\n<h2>Tax and Legal</h2>\n<p>Gig income is generally taxable. Keep earnings records. Check your work visa terms—S Pass and Employment Pass typically allow gig work outside your primary job. Confirm with your employer before starting.</p>\n\n<h2>Pelangi Guests & Gap-Filling</h2>\n<p>Many guests stay with us during that 4–12 week limbo. Those who pick up gig work report three wins: (1) funding relocation costs, (2) staying mentally active, (3) natural downtime when returning to Pelangi. Several guests deliver Foodpanda and work from our common areas.</p>\n\n<h2>Next Steps</h2>\n<p>Choose a gig: Foodpanda for ease, freelancing for scalability, F&B for social connection. <a href=\"/rooms\">Book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> as your base while you earn and prepare for Singapore.</p>\n`,\n  },\n];\n\nexport const blogPosts: BlogPost[] = rawPosts.map((post) => ({\n  ...post,\n  readingTimeMinutes: computeReadingTime(post.content),\n}));\n\nexport function getPostBySlug(slug: string): BlogPost | undefined {\n  return blogPosts.find((post) => post.slug === slug);\n}\n","numLines":84,"startLine":680,"totalLines":763}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_017B1nHRGUirBrL2yFw7fSX6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect! Now I understand the structure. The posts are added to the `rawPosts` array, and then the `blogPosts` export automatically computes the `readingTimeMinutes` based on the HTML content.\n\nKey observations:\n1. The `readTime` field is hardcoded (e.g., \"9 min read\"), but `readingTimeMinutes` is computed\n2. The content is HTML embedded in template literals\n3. Links to /rooms, /book, and /getting-here are common\n4. Recent posts have a date of \"2026-04-14\"\n5. The array ends at line 753, so I need to add the new post before the closing bracket\n\nNow let me create the plan:\n\n## Plan for US-250\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n1. Slug '3-day-jb-backpacker-itinerary-rm250', category 'Backpacker Tips'\n2. Budget HTML table\n3. Pelangi linked to /book\n4. readTime '8 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n5. npm run build passes\n\n**Files to Modify:**\n- `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`: Add a new blog post object to the rawPosts array\n\n**Changes:**\n- Add new post object with:\n  - slug: '3-day-jb-backpacker-itinerary-rm250'\n  - title: 'Blog post: 3-Day JB Backpacker Itinerary Under RM250 (2026)'\n  - category: 'Backpacker Tips'\n  - date: '2026-04-14'\n  - readTime: '8 min read'\n  - author: 'Pelangi Team'\n  - content: Day-by-day itinerary with budget breakdown table (accommodation RM35/night, food RM25/day, transport RM10/day)\n  - Pelangi link to /book\n  - Keep content under 7000 characters\n\nThe post structure will follow a day-by-day breakdown starting with an introduction on why JB appeals to budget travelers, then covering each day's activities (arrival and exploration on Day 1, activities on Days 2-3), meals, and a budget table showing the cost breakdown across accommodation, food, and transport. I'll include a call-to-action linking to the booking page.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3007,"cache_read_input_tokens":88016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3007},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a061755-a806-479f-90eb-b6112c5e5966","uuid":"bfd7372a-3ab3-4b8a-8c3c-f97ffde7b8d4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_017B1nHRGUirBrL2yFw7fSX6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I understand the structure. Let me create a plan before implementing.\n\n## Current State\n- The blog posts are stored as TypeScript objects in `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`\n- Each post includes metadata (slug, title, date, category, readTime) and HTML content\n- The `readingTimeMinutes` is auto-computed from the HTML content\n- Recent posts follow a consistent format with internal links to `/book`, `/rooms`, `/getting-here`\n\n## Problem Identified\nStory US-250 requires adding a new blog post for a 3-day JB backpacker itinerary under RM250, with a day-by-day breakdown and budget table linking Pelangi accommodation at /book.\n\n## Planned Changes\n1. Add new blog post object to the `rawPosts` array in `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`\n2. Include:\n   - slug: '3-day-jb-backpacker-itinerary-rm250'\n   - category: 'Backpacker Tips'\n   - date: '2026-04-14'\n   - readTime: '8 min read'\n   - HTML content with day-by-day itinerary (Days 1-3: morning/afternoon/evening)\n   - Budget table showing: Accommodation (Pelangi RM35/night), Food (RM25/day), Transport (RM10/day)\n   - Link to /book for Pelangi booking\n   - Keep content under 7000 characters\n\n<plan>\n{\n  \"files_to_create\": [],\n  \"files_to_modify\": [\"site/src/data/blog/posts.ts\"],\n  \"functions_to_add\": [],\n  \"estimated_loc\": 140\n}\n</plan>\n\nNow executing the plan:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3007,"cache_read_input_tokens":88016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3007},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2a061755-a806-479f-90eb-b6112c5e5966","uuid":"ef0b6fc4-075a-4d05-8d36-0e379ccf